Ramping V2 – Model Validation Summary
1. Objective

The purpose of the Ramping V2 model is to detect instances of market manipulation through ramping, where a trader intentionally builds or reduces a position in an instrument by placing a series of aggressive trades in the same direction to artificially influence the market price.
Once the price has moved in the desired direction, the trader exits the position—often rapidly—at a profit that would not have been achievable without the price impact of their own trades.
Ramping V2 aims to identify these behaviors by contrasting market-impact-based profit potential (Narrative) against legitimate market-risk exposure (Counter-Narrative).

2. Working Example (Simplified)

Suppose a trader buys $10,000 worth of Instrument X five times consecutively within a short period.
These rapid purchases push the price upward by 2%.
The trader then sells their position at the inflated price, earning an artificial profit.
Without their earlier trades, the market price would have remained stable, and the profit would not exist.
Ramping V2 recognizes this as a ramping pattern because:

The trader executed repeated aggressive buy orders (same direction).

The trades occurred in close succession (short time gap).

The final sell trade benefits directly from the earlier price impact.

3. Component Models Used
Component Model	Description / Function
Market Impact Model V2 (MIM V2)	Estimates price movement attributable to a trader’s own activity; calculates expected mid-price evolution absent manipulation.
Allocation Algorithm	Distinguishes principal vs. client trades and opening vs. closing positions, ensuring ramping logic is applied only to proprietary activity.
Aggression Score Module	Quantifies trade aggressiveness (0–1) based on whether orders cross the bid/ask spread or initiate liquidity taking. Used to scale market impact.
4. Inputs and Parameters

Data Sources – All trade and order data ingested by ETS, including:

Trade identifiers: trader ID, timestamp, buy/sell flag, instrument ID.

Trade direction and size (notional).

Trade aggressiveness (derived from bid/ask crossing).

Price and time-series data for each instrument.

Market-impact constants from MIM V2 calibration.

