Quote Stuffing – Model Validation Summary
1. Objective

The purpose of the Quote Stuffing model is to detect potential instances of high-frequency trading (HFT) abuse in which a trader floods an exchange with a large volume of rapid-fire orders and cancellations.
Such bursts artificially congest exchange systems, creating latency that can be exploited by traders with superior proximity or infrastructure (co-located servers) to profit from short-term price distortions.
Because this activity deliberately induces latency to gain informational or execution advantages, it is considered a form of market manipulation and abuse under market conduct regulations.

2. Working Example

Assume an exchange can normally process 100 messages per millisecond without latency.
A high-frequency trader suddenly submits 2,000 orders and cancels them within milliseconds.
This overwhelms the system, delaying price updates by, say, 10 milliseconds.
During this micro-delay, the same trader—located closest to the exchange—executes a genuine buy order at a stale price before other participants’ systems have received the updated quote.
The trader’s latency advantage creates a temporary and unfair profit opportunity, even though no true supply-demand shift occurred.
The Quote Stuffing metric quantifies such latency-induced advantages in USD Value, representing the potential economic gain attributable to the artificial delay.

3. Component Models Used
Component Model	Function / Purpose
Exchange Capacity Estimation	Determines the exchange’s maximum sustainable order-message throughput (messages per millisecond) before latency occurs, using historical data from sources such as the Consolidated Tape Association (CTA).
Trader Capacity Estimation	Measures an individual trader’s actual message-sending rate to the same exchange and compares it with the exchange’s capacity.
Latency Impact Module	Converts induced delay (milliseconds) into estimated price-movement impact (basis points × instrument notional).

These components feed into the overall USD Value Score, the model’s final output.

4. Inputs and Parameters

The Quote Stuffing metric consumes all trade and order-message data available to ETS, typically including:

Order identifiers: trader ID, timestamp, exchange, instrument ID, order status (submitted/canceled/executed).

Order volume and message count: number of orders, cancels, and total messages per time unit.

Latency metrics: measured processing delay between message submission and acknowledgment.

Exchange capacity reference: historical throughput benchmarks from CTA or similar sources.

Market price data: concurrent quote changes to assess latency-linked price impact.

